 # Camera Matrix Ray Generation
 
-Also called: Ray Generation from Camera Matrix, Ray Generation from Camera Pose, Ray to World Coordinates, Ray Generation from Camera Parameters
+Also called: Ray Generation from Camera Matrix, Ray Generation from Camera Parameters, Ray Generation from Camera Pose, Ray Origin and Direction Transformation, Ray to World Coordinates
 
 ## Overview
-Camera Matrix Ray Generation computes the 3D world-space origin and direction vectors for viewing rays corresponding to each pixel in a raster image, given a camera's intrinsic calibration matrix $K$ and extrinsic camera-to-world transformation matrix $c2w$ [S12, S21, S34, unofficial]. It solves the problem of mapping 2D screen coordinates to 3D rays for volume rendering or ray tracing when the camera is defined by standard computer vision parameters rather than geometric primitives like field of view [S5, S9, unofficial]. Unlike [Pinhole Camera Ray Generation](5058-pinhole-camera-ray-generation.md), which derives rays directly from geometric primitives such as field of view (FOV), aspect ratio, and camera basis vectors, this method relies on explicit intrinsic and extrinsic matrices to handle precise calibration [S12, S21, unofficial].
+Camera Matrix Ray Generation computes the 3D world-space origin and direction vectors for viewing rays corresponding to each pixel in a raster image, given a camera's intrinsic calibration matrix $K$ and extrinsic camera-to-world transformation matrix $c2w$ [S12, S21, S34, unofficial]. It solves the problem of mapping 2D screen coordinates to 3D rays for volume rendering or ray tracing when the camera is defined by standard computer vision parameters rather than geometric primitives like field of view [S5, S9, unofficial]. Unlike [Pinhole Camera Ray Generation](5058-pinhole-camera-ray-generation.md), which derives rays directly from geometric primitives such as field of view (FOV), aspect ratio, and camera basis vectors, or uses a simplified look-at formulation [S1, S2, S9, unofficial], this method relies on explicit intrinsic and extrinsic matrices to handle precise calibration [S12, S21, unofficial].
 
